lly retraced the previous day’s move, while analyst Eric Coleman said price action had become rangebound between horizontal support and resistance until a clear breakout or breakdown occurs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDecrypt’s story on the same theme sharpened the same pressure points with more explicit levels and market structure commentary. The piece said Bitcoin was shrugging off gains tied to the U.S. CLARITY Act because institutions were selling amid surging Treasury yields. Tim Sun, senior researcher at HashKey Group, told Decrypt that funding rates remained generally moderate and the long/short ratio had not reached extremes, but the options market showed a clear resistance zone between $82,000 and $84,000 with downside support at $77,000. Sun warned that if Bitcoin holds the level, ETF outflows would likely create short-term volatility rather than a full trend reversal; if Bitcoin breaks below $77,000 while perpetual swap open interest remains high, the market could enter a deleveraging phase and deepen the decline. Decrypt also quoted Alex Tsepaev, chief strategy officer at B2PRIME Group, who said that when U.S. Treasury yields are above 4.5% and the market prices out future Fed cuts, some allocations naturally shift toward cash and bonds. Tsepaev’s base case was zero rate cuts this year, though one late cut in November or December could still happen if inflation and labor markets weaken. The article added that Myriad users were assigning only a 4% chance that the Fed would cut rates by more than 25 bps before July. On ETF flows, Tsepaev said ETF selling alone would not erase the broader picture but could push Bitcoin back toward the $76,000 to $77,000 area. Decrypt concluded that Bitcoin’s ability to hold above $77,000 would determine whether the outflows remained a short-term headwind or developed into something more damaging.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTaken together, the reporting paints a consistent picture: Bitcoin is under pressure because bond yields, expected Fed policy, and ETF flow dynamics are all leaning against risk assets at the same time. The immediate market focus is on whether BTC can stabilize above the $77,000 support area or whether a further break forces a new round of deleveraging. Across both stories, the $82,000 to $84,000 area is the near-term resistance band, while $76,000 to $77,000 is the zone that keeps showing up as the key downside shelf. The tone in both pieces is cautious but not panicked: analysts are watching for a technical unwind, but they also note that funding rates are not yet extreme and that short-term volatility may still dominate before any larger trend decision is made.\\\\\\\"}. You MUST finish the article completely, ending with the Sources and Word Count block.\\\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nsed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article.md\\n\\nif ! grep -qi \\\"Word Count:\\\" /tmp/crypto-article.md; then\\n  echo \\\"Article incomplete. Forcing recovery run...\\\"\\n  rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\\n  openclaw agent --agent writer --message \\\"Your previous attempt was cut off. Re-read COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md and write a tighter article using these facts: {\\\\\\\"topic_theme\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Bitcoin selloff under $79K as Treasury-yield pressure and ETF outflows weigh on crypto\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"primary_keyword\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Bitcoin selloff\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"primary_headline\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Bitcoin Slides Below $79K as Treasury Yields and ETF Outflows Hit Risk Assets\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"sources_used\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"Cointelegraph\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Decrypt\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"source_urls\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-price-dives-under-79k-us-bond-market-triggers-3-btc-price-rout?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://decrypt.co/367952/bitcoin-shrugs-off-clarity-gains-as-institutions-sell-amid-surging-treasury-yields\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"combined_key_facts\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"