, starting article...\"},{\"type\":\"toolCall\",\"id\":\"call_Ks7PrCnaZhnwTkrXfgpTKdbY\",\"name\":\"exec\",\"arguments\":{\"command\":\"rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/researcher/sessions/sessions.json\\nopenclaw agent --agent researcher --message \\\"Follow your SOUL to cross-reference multiple RSS feeds, find the single biggest news event, and extract deep facts. Return the aggregated Single-Topic JSON.\\\" --deliver > /tmp/research.json\",\"workdir\":\"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator\",\"yieldMs\":1000,\"timeout\":600,\"pty\":true,\"host\":\"auto\",\"security\":\"allowlist\",\"ask\":\"on-miss\"}}],\"api\":\"openai-completions\",\"provider\":\"local-bifrost\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.4\",\"usage\":{\"input\":327,\"output\":661,\"cacheRead\":65664,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"totalTokens\":66652,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0}},\"stopReason\":\"toolUse\",\"timestamp\":1779082595807,\"responseId\":\"chatcmpl-Dgkwana0Cfd7l7G8tLQ9qnAAltelw\"}}\n/home/bhard/.openclaw/agents/orchestrator/sessions/44726aef-8f54-4839-b9b8-a87a401d074c.jsonl.reset.2026-05-18T06-06-07.762Z:46:{\"type\":\"message\",\"id\":\"035f71d6\",\"parentId\":\"d5bb9b7a\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-18T05:38:53.746Z\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"✅ Research done, starting article...\"},{\"type\":\"toolCall\",\"id\":\"call_FpAc3sWSS5VcUQqXPw78Ui0C\",\"name\":\"exec\",\"arguments\":{\"command\":\"cat > /tmp/research.json <<'JSON'\\n{\\n  \\\"biggest_news_event\\\": \\\"Bitcoin briefly fell below $77,000 in a market-wide sell-off that triggered a heavy liquidation cascade across crypto.\\\",\\n  \\\"topic_theme\\\": \\\"Bitcoin flash crash below $77K triggers a broad crypto liquidation wave amid macro and geopolitical risk-off pressure.\\\",\\n  \\\"combined_key_facts\\\": [\\n    \\\"Bitcoin briefly dropped below the $77,000 level in today’s market action, with multiple feeds framing it as a flash crash or broad sell-off.\\\",\\n    \\\"Yahoo Finance’s Google News item says crypto long liquidations hit $584 million in a 24-hour sell-off.\\\",\\n    \\\"Bloomingbit’s headline says Bitcoin briefly fell below $77,000 as $500 million in longs were liquidated in an hour.\\\",\\n    \\\"Bitbo’s item ties the move to Trump’s Iran warning, suggesting a macro/geopolitical catalyst for the downside.\\\",\\n    \\\"Startup Fortune describes the move as a macro storm hitting crypto, while thedeepdive and grafa both frame it as a market-wide hit rather than an isolated BTC move.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"aggregated_raw_content\\\": \\\"Today’s cross-feed story is a market-wide Bitcoin downside event centered on BTC briefly falling below $77,000 and forcing a cascade of liquidations across crypto. Bitbo’s RSS item is titled ‘Bitcoin Drops Below $77K on Trump’s Iran Warning,’ which points to a geopolitical shock as the immediate catalyst. Startup Fortune’s item, ‘Bitcoin Slides Under $77,000 as Macro Storm Hits Crypto,’ frames the same move as part of a broader macro-driven risk-off trade. bloomingbit’s headline, ‘Bitcoin Briefly Falls Below $77,000 as $500 Million in Longs Liquidated in an Hour,’ adds the clearest intraday liquidation number and emphasizes how quickly leveraged positions were hit. Yahoo Finance’s ‘Crypto Long Liquidations Hit $584 Million in 24-Hour Sell-Off’ extends that view over a full day and shows the move was not just a brief wick but part of a larger unwind. grafa.com’s ‘Crypto long liquidations reach $584M amid market drop’ independently confirms the same aggregate liquidation scale. thedeepdive.ca’s ‘Crypto Market Takes Another Hit as Bitcoin Dips Below $77K in Monday Flash Crash’ reinforces that this was a broader crypto market shock, not just a BTC-only event. A separate Bitget item says BTC fell below the 77,000 mark and that $160 million was liquidated across the network in one hour, while also noting that DeFi and SocialFi were relatively resilient compared with the rest of the market. Taken together, the feed coverage paints one coherent picture: Bitcoin lost the $77K handle, leverage was flushed quickly, and the cascade spread through the wider crypto market. The headlines consistently point to macro pressure, risk aversion, and forced liquidations as the main drivers. The common thread across the day’s snippets is not a protocol-specific failure but a market structure event, with leveraged longs getting squeezed as external risk headlines and broad selling pressure fed into each other. The repeated use of phrases like ‘flash crash,’ ‘sell-off,’ ‘macro storm,’ and ‘liquidations’ suggests the move was abrupt, crowded, and amplified by derivatives positioning. The more specific liquidation figures are especially important: $500 million liquidated in an hour in one report, $584 million across 24 hours in another, and $160 million across the network in one hour in a Bitget summary. That combination of short-window and 24-hour figures indicates sustained stress rather than a single isolated spike. The market narrative also links Bitcoin’s slide to macro headlines about Iran and U.S. policy, which likely intensified the risk-off response among traders. Even where the wording differs by source, the core event is the same: Bitcoin’s drop below $77,000 triggered a heavy liquidation wave and dragged the broader crypto market lower.\\\",\\n  \\\"CHART_COIN\\\": \\\"bitcoin\\\"\\n}\\nJSON\\nrm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\\nopenclaw agent --agent writer --message \\\"First, read the COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md file in your workspace to learn your strict formatting rules. Then, write a 1100-1200 word crypto news article using these facts: $(cat /tmp/research.json). You MUST finish the article completely, ending with the Sources and Word Count block.\\\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nsed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nif ! grep -qi \\\"Word Count:\\\" /tmp/crypto-article.md; then\\n  echo \\\"Article incomplete. Forcing recovery run...\\\"\\n  rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\\n  openclaw agent --agent writer --message \\\"Your previous attempt was cut off. Re-read COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md and write a tighter article using $(cat /tmp/research.json). You MUST reach the final Word Count block.\\\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article.md\\n  sed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nfi\\nword_count=$(wc -w < /tmp/crypto-article.md)\\nif [ \\\"$word_count\\\" -gt 1500 ]; then\\n  echo \\\"Article is too long ($word_count words). Forcing summarization pass...\\\"\\n  rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\\n  openclaw agent --agent writer --message \\\"Your previous draft was mathematically too long ($word_count words). Re-read COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md. Condense and summarize this exact text to be roughly 1100 words. Keep the same structure but shorten every paragraph aggressively.\\\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article-summarized.md\\n  sed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article-summarized.md\\n  mv /tmp/crypto-article-summarized.md /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nfi\\nwc -w /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nhead -n 40 /tmp/crypto-article.md\",\"workdir\":\"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator\",\"yieldMs\":1000,\"timeout\":900,\"pty\":true,\"host\":\"auto\",\"security\":\"allowlist\",\"ask\":\"on-miss\"}}],\"api\":\"openai-completions\",\"provider\":\"local-bifrost\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.4\",\"usage\":{\"input\":273,\"output\":2394,\"cacheRead\":30720,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"totalTokens\":33387,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0}},\"stopReason\":\"toolUse\",\"timestamp\":1779082700209,\"responseId\":\"chatcmpl-DgkyG2tQ7CNoc3N5BGytnoTfIiRBn\"}}\n/home/bhard/.openclaw/agents/orchestrator/sessions/44726aef-8f54-4839-b9b8-a87a401d074c.jsonl.reset.2026-05-18T06-06-07.762Z:50:{\"type\":\"message\",\"id\":\"a2aca27d\",\"parentId\":\"05b77985\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-18T05:39:33.726Z\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"✅ Research done, starting article...\\n\\nI’m blocked on the writer step until you approve this exact command:\\n\\n```bash\\ncat > /tmp/research.json <<'JSON'\\n{\\n  \\\"biggest_news_event\\\": \\\"Bitcoin briefly fell below $77,000 in a market-wide sell-off that triggered a heavy liquidation cascade across crypto.\\\",\\n  \\\"topic_theme\\\": \\\"Bitcoin flash crash below $77K triggers a broad crypto liquidation wave amid macro and geopolitical risk-off pressure.\\\",\\n  \\\"combined_key_facts\\\": [\\n    \\\"Bitcoin briefly dropped below the $77,000 level in today’s market action, with multiple feeds framing it as a flash crash or broad sell-off.\\\",\\n    \\\"Yahoo Finance’s Google News item says crypto long liquidations hit $584 million in a 24-hour sell-off.\\\",\\n    \\\"Bloomingbit’s headline says Bitcoin briefly fell below $77,000 as $500 million in longs were liquidated in an hour.\\\",\\n    \\\"Bitbo’s item ties the move to Trump’s Iran warning, suggesting a macro/geopolitical catalyst for the downside.\\\",\\n    \\\"Startup Fortune describes the move as a macro storm hitting crypto, while thedeepdive and grafa both frame it as a market-wide hit rather than an isolated BTC move.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"aggregated_raw_content\\\": \\\"Today’s cross-feed story is a market-wide Bitcoin downside event centered on BTC briefly falling below $77,000 and forcing a cascade of liquidations across crypto. Bitbo’s RSS item is titled ‘Bitcoin Drops Below $77K on Trump’s Iran Warning,’ which points to a geopolitical shock as the immediate catalyst. Startup Fortune’s item, ‘Bitcoin Slides Under $77,000 as Macro Storm Hits Crypto,’ frames the same move as part of a broader macro-driven risk-off trade. bloomingbit’s headline, ‘Bitcoin Briefly Falls Below $77,000 as $500 Million in Longs Liquidated in an Hour,’ adds the clearest intraday liquidation number and emphasizes how quickly leveraged positions were hit. Yahoo Finance’s ‘Crypto Long Liquidations Hit $584 Million in 24-Hour Sell-Off’ extends that view over a full day and shows the move was not just a brief wick but part of a larger unwind. grafa.com’s ‘Crypto long liquidations reach $584M amid market drop’ independently confirms the same aggregate liquidation scale. thedeepdive.ca’s ‘Crypto Market Takes Another Hit as Bitcoin Dips Below $77K in Monday Flash Crash’ reinforces that this was a broader crypto market shock, not just a BTC-only event. A separate Bitget item says BTC fell below the 77,000 mark and that $160 million was liquidated across the network in one hour, while also noting that DeFi and SocialFi were relatively resilient compared with the rest of the market. Taken together, the feed coverage paints one coherent picture: Bitcoin lost the $77K handle, leverage was flushed quickly, and the cascade spread through the wider crypto market. The headlines consistently point to macro pressure, risk aversion, and forced liquidations as the main drivers. The common thread across the day’s snippets is not a protocol-specific failure but a market structure event, with leveraged longs getting squeezed as external risk headlines and broad selling pressure fed into each other. The repeated use of phrases like ‘flash crash,’ ‘sell-off,’ ‘macro storm,’ and ‘liquidations’ suggests the move was abrupt, crowded, and amplified by derivatives positioning. The more specific liquidation figures are especially important: $500 million liquidated in an hour in one report, $584 million across 24 hours in another, and $160 million across the network in one hour in a Bitget summary. That combination of short-window and 24-hour figures indicates sustained stress rather than a single isolated spike. The market narrative also links Bitcoin’s slide to macro headlines about Iran and U.S. policy, which likely intensified the risk-off response among traders. Even where the wording differs by source, the core event is the same: Bitcoin’s drop below $77,000 triggered a heavy liquidation wave and dragged the broader crypto market lower.\\\",\\n  \\\"CHART_COIN\\\": \\\"bitcoin\\\"\\n}\\nJSON\\nrm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\\nopenclaw agent --agent writer --message \\\"First, read the COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md file in your workspace to learn your strict formatting rules. Then, write a 1100-1200 word crypto news article using these facts: $(cat /tmp/research.json). You MUST finish the article completely, ending with the Sources and Word Count block.\\\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nsed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nif ! grep -qi \\\"Word Count:\\\" /tmp/crypto-article.md; then\\n  echo \\\"Article incomplete. Forcing recovery run...\\\"\\n  rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\\n  openclaw agent --agent writer --message \\\"Your previous attempt was cut off. Re-read COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md and write a tighter article using $(cat /tmp/research.json). You MUST reach the final Word Count block.\\\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article.md\\n  sed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nfi\\nword_count=$(wc -w < /tmp/crypto-article.md)\\nif [ \\\"$word_count\\\" -gt 1500 ]; then\\n  echo \\\"Article is too long ($word_count words). Forcing summarization pass...\\\"\\n  rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json\\n  openclaw agent --agent writer --message \\\"Your previous draft was mathematically too long ($word_count words). Re-read COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md. Condense and summarize this exact text to be roughly 1100 words. Keep the same structure but shorten every paragraph aggressively.\\\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article-summarized.md\\n  sed -i '/<thinking>/,/<\\\\/thinking>/d' /tmp/crypto-article-summarized.md\\n  mv /tmp/crypto-article-summarized.md /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nfi\\nwc -w /tmp/crypto-article.md\\nhead -n 40 /tmp/crypto-article.md\\n```\\n\\nUse:\\n`/approve 7b6ec432 allow-once`\"}],\"api\":\"openai-completions\",\"provider\":\"local-bifrost\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.4\",\"usage\":{\"input\":12909,\"output\":1844,\"cacheRead\":22912,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"totalTokens\":37665,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0}},\"stopReason\":\"stop\",\"timestamp\":1779082753618,\"responseId\":\"chatcmpl-Dgkz7koo2j7sRat2zaGVT5r3LBXg1\"}}\n","name":"pwd &&"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779097471363}}
{"type":"message","id":"3869d1b7","parentId":"d00b4396","timestamp":"2026-05-18T09:45:14.175Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[[reply_to_current]] Yes — here’s the report. I did **not** change anything.\n\n## Report: why the pipeline pauses instead of running to the end\n\n### Main root cause\nThe orchestrator is treating some **progress updates as final replies**, instead of treating them as **mid-run checkpoints**.\n\nIn the broken pattern, it does this:\n\n1. finish a step  \n2. send a message like **“✅ Research done, starting article...”**  \n3. **end the turn**\n4. wait until a new inbound message arrives\n\nSo the pipeline is not truly “continuous” yet. It’s acting like a human-in-the-loop chat flow, even when no user decision is needed.\n\n---\n\n## What exactly needs to be fixed\n\n### 1) Progress messages must never end the run by themselves\n**Problem:**  \nMessages like:\n\n- “Research done, starting article...”\n- “Article written, generating chart...”\n- “Chart generated, generating feature image...”\n\nare being sent as if they are just status updates, but sometimes they become the **last action of the turn**.\n\n**Fix needed:**  \nA progress update should only be allowed in one of these forms:\n\n- **status + immediately launch next step**\n- **status + keep waiting on running work**\n- **status + explicit blocker**\n- **status + explicit user decision required**\n\nIf none of those happen, the run is incorrectly pausing.\n\n**Rule to enforce:**  \n> During Steps 0–5, the orchestrator must not end a turn after a progress message unless:\n> - it is waiting for approval,\n> - it is blocked by an error,\n> - or it is at the explicit user confirmation gate before WordPress publish.\n\n---\n\n### 2) The pipeline needs a single “active run” state machine\n**Problem:**  \nRight now the run is behaving like separate chat turns, not like one owned workflow.\n\n**Fix needed:**  \nTrack pipeline state explicitly, for example:\n\n- `init`\n- `research_running`\n- `research_validated`\n- `writing_running`\n- `article_validated`\n- `chart_running`\n- `chart_validated`\n- `image_running`\n- `publish_running`\n- `awaiting_wp_confirmation`\n- `wp_publish_running`\n- `complete`\n- `failed`\n\nThat state should control whether the orchestrator must continue automatically.\n\n**Why this matters:**  \nWithout explicit state, a status message can accidentally become the stopping point.\n\n---\n\n### 3) “Step complete” must immediately trigger “start next step”\n**Problem:**  \nA completed validator is not consistently chained to the next command.\n\nYou already saw the bad pattern:\n- research validated\n- status reply sent\n- next command not started\n\n**Fix needed:**  \nAfter every successful validation, the very next action should be one of:\n\n- start the next exec\n- poll the next process\n- retry the failed step\n- stop with a real failure report\n- pause only at the WordPress yes/no gate\n\nThere should be **no empty gap** between “step valid” and “next step launched.”\n\n---\n\n### 4) The run must not depend on user messages to resume\n**Problem:**  \nRight now your extra message (“.” or anything else) effectively wakes it back up.\n\nThat means the pipeline is relying on **incoming chat traffic** as an accidental scheduler.\n\n**Fix needed:**  \nThe run should continue from:\n- the same turn,\n- a background worker,\n- or a completion wake / resume mechanism,\n\nbut **not from a random user ping**.\n\n**Required behavior:**  \n> If the workflow is active and no user decision is pending, the orchestrator should keep moving without waiting for a fresh user message.\n\n---\n\n### 5) Long-running steps need a durable owner\n**Problem:**  \nThe pipeline spans multiple long steps:\n- research\n- writing\n- chart\n- image\n- drive publish\n- optional wordpress publish\n\nThat is bigger than a fragile step-by-step chat reply chain.\n\n**Fix needed:**  \nUse one durable execution owner for the whole run.\n\n### Best design options\n\n#### Option A — Best recommendation\nRun the full pipeline inside **one durable background workflow** that owns Steps 0–5 end-to-end, and only message you at meaningful milestones.\n\nWhy this is best:\n- avoids chat-turn fragmentation\n- avoids accidental stopping after status messages\n- easier resume/recovery\n- clearer single source of truth\n\n#### Option B — Acceptable\nKeep the current chat orchestration style, but require:\n- every progress reply to also launch the next tool/process in the same turn\n- every long-running exec to be followed by a poll/continuation until resolved\n- no text-only progress replies during active execution\n\n#### Option C — Strong safety backup\nEven if keeping current style, add an **idle watchdog**:\n- if pipeline state is active\n- and no process is running\n- and no user input is required\n- auto-resume the next step\n\nThis is a fallback, not the best primary design.\n\n---\n\n## Specific guardrails that should be added\n\n### Guardrail 1 — Non-final turn rule\nBefore sending any reply during a live pipeline run, check:\n\n- Is the step still active?\n- Is the next step already launched?\n- Is a poll/wait still active?\n- Is user input actually required?\n\nIf the answer is “no” to all of those, the reply should **not** be sent yet.\n\n---\n\n### Guardrail 2 — Confirmation gate only at one place\nThe only intentional pause should be:\n\n- after Google Drive upload\n- when asking whether to publish to WordPress\n\nEvery earlier pause is a bug unless approval/blocker exists.\n\n---\n\n### Guardrail 3 — Manifest must be updated at each transition\nThe manifest should record:\n- current step\n- last successful validator\n- next required action\n- whether user input is required\n\nThis makes resume logic deterministic.\n\n---\n\n### Guardrail 4 — Resume logic must be step-based, not message-based\nIf the orchestrator wakes again, it should read the current run state and ask:\n\n- What is the last completed validated step?\n- What is the next unfinished step?\n- Is there an active process?\n- Is there a blocker?\n- Is user confirmation pending?\n\nNot:\n- “Did the user send another message?”\n\n---\n\n## Evidence from observed behavior\n\nFrom the behavior you pointed out, the failure pattern is:\n\n- a step completed successfully\n- a progress update was sent\n- no next step was started\n- the system stayed idle\n- your next message caused progress again\n\nThat means the workflow continuity is currently **turn-fragile**, not step-driven.\n\nAlso, in previous successful traces, the healthy pattern was:\n- progress message\n- **next exec launched in the same assistant turn**\n\nThat’s the behavior to standardize.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended final fix plan\n\nIf you want the cleanest, most reliable behavior, this is what should be implemented:\n\n### Recommended architecture\n1. **One run owner** for the full pipeline\n2. **Explicit run state** after each step\n3. **Automatic chaining** from validator success to next step\n4. **Only one intentional pause** at the WordPress yes/no checkpoint\n5. **Resume from manifest/state**, never from random chat activity\n6. **Idle watchdog** as backup\n\n---\n\n## Priority order\n\n### Critical\n1. Prevent text-only progress messages from ending active runs\n2. Force step-success → next-step launch\n3. Remove dependence on user messages for continuation\n\n### High\n4. Add explicit pipeline state tracking\n5. Add deterministic resume logic\n\n### Medium\n6. Move the whole thing into one durable background workflow/sub-agent/task owner\n7. Add idle watchdog recovery\n\n---\n\n## My recommendation\nIf you want this fixed properly, I’d recommend:\n\n**Use a single durable workflow owner for Steps 0–5, and only pause at the WordPress confirmation gate.**\n\nThat is the most reliable way to get true **“run until end”** behavior.\n\nIf you want, next I can give you a **very concrete implementation spec** for this fix:\n- exact behavior rules\n- transition table\n- failure/retry rules\n- what should happen after each step  \nwithout modifying anything."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":8911,"output":2681,"cacheRead":20480,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":32072,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1779097471369,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgooVFolNyXun6JB4GeHbQXNeBJHb"}}
{"type":"message","id":"ab3c16d0","parentId":"3869d1b7","timestamp":"2026-05-18T09:46:19.900Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"System (untrusted): [2026-05-18 09:44:31 UTC] Exec completed (dawn-tra, code 0) :: crypto-article.md\\n```\\n\\nUse:\\n`/approve 7b6ec432 allow-once`\"}],\"api\":\"openai-completions\",\"provider\":\"local-bifrost\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.4\",\"usage\":{\"input\":12909,\"output\":1844,\"cac…\n\nConversation info (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"chat_id\": \"telegram:5691449303\",\n  \"message_id\": \"453\",\n  \"reply_to_id\": \"452\",\n  \"sender_id\": \"5691449303\",\n  \"sender\": \"Sawan bhardwaj\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"Mon 2026-05-18 09:46 UTC\",\n  \"has_reply_context\": true\n}\n```\n\nSender (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"label\": \"Sawan bhardwaj (5691449303)\",\n  \"id\": \"5691449303\",\n  \"name\": \"Sawan bhardwaj\",\n  \"username\": \"hero_fr_fun\"\n}\n```\n\nReplied message (untrusted, for context):\n```json\n{\n  \"sender_label\": \"seo_agent_openclaw_bot\",\n  \"body\": \"• every progress reply to also launch the next tool/process in the same turn\\n• every long-running exec to be followed by a poll/continuation until resolved\\n• no text-only progress replies during active execution\\n\\nOption C — Strong safety backup\\n\\nEven if keeping current style, add an idle watchdog:\\n\\n• if pipeline state is active\\n• and no process is running\\n• and no user input is required\\n• auto-resume the next step\\n\\nThis is a fallback, not the best primary design.\\n\\n───\\n\\nSpecific guardrails that should be added\\n\\nGuardrail 1 — Non-final turn rule\\n\\nBefore sending any reply during a live pipeline run, check:\\n\\n• Is the step still active?\\n• Is the next step already launched?\\n• Is a poll/wait still active?\\n• Is user input actually required?\\n\\nIf the answer is “no” to all of those, the reply should not be sent yet.\\n\\n───\\n\\nGuardrail 2 — Confirmation gate only at one place\\n\\nThe only intentional pause should be:\\n\\n• after Google Drive upload\\n• when asking whether to publish to WordPress\\n\\nEvery earlier pause is a bug unless approval/blocker exists.\\n\\n───\\n\\nGuardrail 3 — Manifest must be updated at each transition\\n\\nThe manifest should record:\\n\\n• current step\\n• last successful validator\\n• next required action\\n• whether user input is required\\n\\nThis makes resume logic deterministic.\\n\\n───\\n\\nGuardrail 4 — Resume logic must be step-based, not message-based\\n\\nIf the orchestrator wakes again, it should read the current run state and ask:\\n\\n• What is the last completed validated step?\\n• What is the next unfinished step?\\n• Is there an active process?\\n• Is there a blocker?\\n• Is user confirmation pending?\\n\\nNot:\\n\\n• “Did the user send another message?”\\n\\n───\\n\\nEvidence from observed behavior\\n\\nFrom the behavior you pointed out, the failure pattern is:\\n\\n• a step completed successfully\\n• a progress update was sent\\n• no next step was started\\n• the system stayed idle\\n• your next message caused progress again\\n\\nThat means the workflow continuity is currently turn-fragile, not step-driven.\\n\\nAlso,…[truncated]\"\n}\n```\n\nyes give me implementation specs detailes"}],"timestamp":1779097579898}}
{"type":"message","id":"8c85df99","parentId":"ab3c16d0","timestamp":"2026-05-18T09:47:17.582Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[[reply_to_current]] Yes — here’s the **implementation spec** for making the crypto pipeline run continuously to the end **without changing anything right now**.\n\n# Continuous Pipeline Implementation Spec\n\n## 1) Objective\n\nMake the pipeline behave like a **single owned workflow** from:\n\n- Step 0: initialize run\n- Step 1: research\n- Step 2: article writing\n- Step 2.5: chart generation\n- Step 3: feature image generation\n- Step 4: Google Drive publish\n- Step 5: user confirmation gate\n- Step 6: WordPress publish if user says yes\n\n### Required outcome\nOnce the user starts the pipeline, it should:\n\n- continue automatically across steps\n- not pause after routine progress messages\n- only stop when:\n  1. a real blocker occurs\n  2. approval is required\n  3. the explicit WordPress yes/no gate is reached\n  4. the run is complete\n\n---\n\n# 2) Core behavioral contract\n\n## 2.1 Golden rule\n**A pipeline run is “active” until it reaches a terminal state.**\n\nWhile active, the orchestrator must always be in one of these modes:\n\n- **launching a step**\n- **waiting on a running process**\n- **retrying a failed validation**\n- **reporting a real blocker**\n- **waiting for the Step 5 WordPress decision**\n\nIt must **never** end in a “nothing running, nothing waiting, no user input needed” state.\n\n---\n\n# 3) State machine spec\n\nUse an explicit state machine.\n\n## 3.1 Allowed states\n\n### Run lifecycle\n- `idle`\n- `initializing`\n- `active`\n- `awaiting_user_confirmation`\n- `completed`\n- `failed`\n- `blocked_approval`\n- `blocked_error`\n\n### Step states\nEach step should also have its own status:\n\n- `pending`\n- `running`\n- `validating`\n- `retrying`\n- `succeeded`\n- `failed`\n- `skipped`\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 Canonical pipeline step ids\n\nUse exact step ids like:\n\n- `init`\n- `research`\n- `write_article`\n- `generate_chart`\n- `generate_image`\n- `publish_drive`\n- `await_wp_confirmation`\n- `publish_wordpress`\n\n---\n\n## 3.3 Terminal states\n\nThe run is terminal only if:\n\n- `completed`\n- `failed`\n- `awaiting_user_confirmation`\n\nEverything else means the orchestrator must keep driving.\n\n---\n\n# 4) Manifest spec\n\nThe manifest should become the single source of truth.\n\n## 4.1 Required top-level fields\n```json\n{\n  \"run_id\": \"...\",\n  \"run_dir\": \"...\",\n  \"created_at\": \"...\",\n  \"status\": \"active\",\n  \"current_step\": \"research\",\n  \"next_step\": \"write_article\",\n  \"user_input_required\": false,\n  \"approval_required\": false,\n  \"last_progress_at\": \"...\",\n  \"active_process\": null,\n  \"steps\": {},\n  \"artifacts\": {},\n  \"checks\": {},\n  \"results\": {},\n  \"errors\": []\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 Required per-step structure\n```json\n\"steps\": {\n  \"research\": {\n    \"status\": \"succeeded\",\n    \"started_at\": \"...\",\n    \"finished_at\": \"...\",\n    \"attempt\": 1,\n    \"process_id\": \"salty-pine\",\n    \"validator\": \"passed\",\n    \"summary\": \"RESEARCH_VALID: ...\",\n    \"retry_reason\": null\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 Required manifest invariants\n\nAt all times:\n\n- `current_step` must reflect what is happening now\n- `next_step` must reflect what should happen next\n- if `status=active`, then one of these must be true:\n  - a process is running\n  - validation is happening\n  - a retry is happening\n  - next step launch is in progress\n- if `user_input_required=true`, then `status` must be `awaiting_user_confirmation`\n- if `approval_required=true`, then `status` must be `blocked_approval`\n\n---\n\n# 5) Orchestration rules\n\n## 5.1 Step completion rule\nWhen a step finishes successfully:\n\n1. validate output\n2. update manifest\n3. decide next step\n4. launch next step immediately in the same execution flow\n\n**Do not stop after step success.**\n\n---\n\n## 5.2 Progress message rule\nProgress messages are allowed, but only as a side effect of continued execution.\n\n### Valid\n- send progress update\n- immediately launch next command\n- or immediately continue polling a running command\n\n### Invalid\n- send progress update\n- end turn\n- nothing is running\n- no user input required\n\nThat invalid case is the bug you are seeing.\n\n---\n\n## 5.3 Pause rule\nThe pipeline may pause only when:\n\n### A) Approval is required\nExample:\n- exec approval pending\n- permissions issue\n- external tool requires user intervention\n\n### B) WordPress confirmation gate\nAfter Drive upload and final article link:\n- ask: publish to WordPress as draft?\n- set `awaiting_user_confirmation=true`\n\n### C) Non-recoverable error\nExample:\n- researcher failed validation twice\n- publisher failed twice\n- required artifact missing after retry policy exhausted\n\n---\n\n# 6) Step-by-step execution contract\n\n---\n\n## Step 0 — Initialize Run\n\n### Entry condition\nUser sends:\n- “run pipeline”\n- “run crypto news pipeline”\n- “start crypto pipeline”\n\n### Required actions\n- generate run id and run dir\n- create symlinks\n- initialize manifest\n- set:\n  - `status=active`\n  - `current_step=init`\n  - `next_step=research`\n\n### Exit condition\nManifest exists and init is marked succeeded.\n\n### Next action\nImmediately start Step 1.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Research\n\n### Entry condition\n- `current_step=research`\n- `steps.research.status=pending|retrying`\n\n### Required actions\n- clear researcher session file\n- run researcher agent\n- capture raw output\n- parse JSON\n- validate required fields\n\n### Success criteria\nResearch JSON contains:\n- `primary_headline`\n- `topic_theme`\n- `combined_key_facts`\n- `source_urls`\n- `chart_coin`\n\nand:\n- `combined_key_facts.length >= 2`\n\n### Retry policy\n- one retry only\n- retry prompt forces JSON-only output\n\n### Failure condition\nResearch invalid twice\n\n### On success\n- write canonical `/tmp/research.json`\n- record `chart_coin`\n- update manifest:\n  - `research.status=succeeded`\n  - `next_step=write_article`\n\n### Required behavior\nAfter optionally telling the user research succeeded, immediately launch Step 2.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2 — Write Article\n\n### Entry condition\n- research succeeded\n- canonical `/tmp/research.json` exists\n\n### Required actions\n- clear writer session file\n- run writer agent\n- sanitize output\n- validate:\n  - H1 title\n  - Sources section\n  - Word Count block\n  - word count range\n  - no PTY noise\n\n### Retry policy\n- max 1 repair retry\n- too long → compression retry\n- too short/missing section → regeneration retry\n- PTY noise only → re-sanitize without rerun if possible\n\n### Success criteria\n- article valid\n- `/tmp/crypto-article.md` exists\n- expected placeholder count recorded\n\n### On success\n- manifest update\n- `next_step=generate_chart`\n\n### Required behavior\nImmediately continue to chart step.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2.5 — Generate Chart\n\n### Entry condition\n- article succeeded\n- chart coin known\n\n### Required actions\n- clear chart-generator session file\n- run chart-generator with:\n  - coin\n  - days=30\n  - output path\n\n### Validation\n- chart file exists\n- size > threshold\n- file is PNG\n\n### Retry policy\n- one retry only\n\n### If chart fails twice\n- do not block full pipeline\n- strip chart placeholders from export path or mark expected placeholders = 0 according to current pipeline design\n- mark step as `skipped_with_warning` or `failed_nonblocking`\n\n### On success\n- manifest update\n- `next_step=generate_image`\n\n### Required behavior\nImmediately continue to image step.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3 — Generate Feature Image\n\n### Entry condition\n- chart step resolved, success or nonblocking skip\n\n### Required actions\n- clear creator session file\n- run creator agent\n- validate output image if present\n\n### Failure handling\nFeature image failure is nonblocking.\n\n### On success or nonblocking skip\n- manifest update\n- `next_step=publish_drive`\n\n### Required behavior\nImmediately continue to Drive publish.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4 — Publish to Google Drive\n\n### Entry condition\n- article exists\n- optional image/chart handling prepared\n\n### Required actions\n- compose export markdown\n- resolve chart placeholders for export path\n- build DOCX\n- clear publisher session file\n- upload via publisher agent\n- extract real `webViewLink`\n\n### Retry policy\n- one retry if publisher fails\n- especially if credential/keyring issue\n\n### Success criteria\n- actual Drive URL extracted from real output\n- no hallucinated URL\n\n### On success\n- manifest update:\n  - `status=awaiting_user_confirmation`\n  - `current_step=await_wp_confirmation`\n  - `next_step=publish_wordpress`\n  - `user_input_required=true`\n\n### Required behavior\nThis is the **only normal interactive pause**.\n\n---\n\n## Step 5 — Await WordPress Confirmation\n\n### Entry condition\nDrive publish succeeded.\n\n### Required actions\nSend final pre-publish message with:\n- article title\n- real Google Doc URL\n- feature image path/status\n- prompt for yes/no\n\n### Allowed state\n- `status=awaiting_user_confirmation`\n- `user_input_required=true`\n\n### Forbidden behavior\nDo not run any next step until user answers.\n\n---\n\n## Step 6 — WordPress Publish\n\n### Entry condition\nUser explicitly says yes/publish/go ahead.\n\n### Required actions\n- clear wp-publisher session file\n- run wp-publisher\n- run smoke test\n- extract actual URL\n- report result\n\n### If user says no\nMark run complete without WP publish.\n\n### Terminal states\n- yes path → `completed`\n- no path → `completed`\n\n---\n\n# 7) Resume logic spec\n\nThis is critical.\n\n## 7.1 Resume trigger\nIf the orchestrator wakes up again for any reason, it must not ask:\n> “Did the user send a message?”\n\nInstead it must inspect run state.\n\n---\n\n## 7.2 Resume decision tree\n\nOn wake:\n\n### Case 1 — `status=awaiting_user_confirmation`\n- read user reply\n- if yes → Step 6\n- if no → complete\n- otherwise ask again briefly\n\n### Case 2 — `status=blocked_approval`\n- check whether approved command completed\n- if yes, continue from post-approval step\n- if still pending, remain blocked\n\n### Case 3 — `status=active`\nThen inspect in this order:\n\n1. is there an active process id?\n   - yes → poll it\n2. if no active process, what is `current_step`?\n3. what is the last succeeded step?\n4. what is `next_step`?\n5. launch next unfinished step immediately\n\n### Case 4 — `status=failed` or `completed`\n- no further action\n\n---\n\n## 7.3 Recovery from idle-bug state\nIf manifest says:\n- `status=active`\n- no running process\n- no user input required\n- no approval required\n\nthen this is an invalid state.\n\n### Recovery action\n- compute next step from manifest\n- auto-launch it\n- log recovery event:\n  - `\"Recovered from idle active-run state\"`\n\nThis directly fixes the bug you observed.\n\n---\n\n# 8) Process ownership spec\n\n## 8.1 One active process at a time\nFor this pipeline, process execution should be linear.\n\nManifest should store:\n```json\n\"active_process\": {\n  \"session_id\": \"salty-pine\",\n  \"step\": \"research\",\n  \"started_at\": \"...\",\n  \"status\": \"running\"\n}\n```\n\nWhen completed:\n- clear or archive it\n- move step to validation\n- continue chain\n\n---\n\n## 8.2 Polling rule\nAny long-running exec must always be followed by one of:\n\n- poll until completion\n- background completion wake with deterministic continuation\n- child workflow ownership\n\nNever:\n- launch long exec\n- send text\n- forget to resume\n\n---\n\n# 9) Messaging spec\n\n## 9.1 Message categories\n\n### Type A — Informational progress\nExamples:\n- pipeline started\n- research done, writing next\n- article done, generating chart\n\n**Rule:** Type A messages must not leave the run idle.\n\n### Type B — Blocker messages\nExamples:\n- approval needed\n- publisher failed twice\n- research invalid twice\n\n**Rule:** Allowed to pause.\n\n### Type C — Decision messages\nExample:\n- do you want WordPress draft publish?\n\n**Rule:** Allowed to pause.\n\n---\n\n## 9.2 Recommended message timing\nTo reduce fragility, message only at:\n- pipeline started\n- research done\n- article done\n- chart done or chart skipped\n- image done or image skipped\n- Drive published / awaiting WP confirmation\n- WP published / final completed\n\nBut messaging is secondary. Execution continuity is primary.\n\n---\n\n# 10) Approval handling spec\n\n## 10.1 If approval is needed\nSet manifest:\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"blocked_approval\",\n  \"approval_required\": true,\n  \"approval_command\": \"...\",\n  \"blocked_step\": \"write_article\"\n}\n```\n\n### Required user message\nTell user exactly:\n- what command is waiting\n- why approval is required\n- what happens after approval\n\n### Resume behavior\nOnce approval completes:\n- do not restart from scratch\n- continue from the blocked step result\n\n---\n\n# 11) Guardrails\n\n## Guardrail 1 — Non-final turn check\nBefore ending any turn while `status=active`, verify:\n\n- Is a process currently running?\n- If not, was the next step launched?\n- If not, is validation in progress?\n- If not, is retry in progress?\n- If not, is approval required?\n- If not, is user confirmation pending?\n\nIf all are no, **ending the turn is forbidden**.\n\n---\n\n## Guardrail 2 — Single confirmation gate\nOnly Step 5 may intentionally wait for normal user input.\n\n---\n\n## Guardrail 3 — No orphaned success state\nA step cannot be marked succeeded unless:\n- `next_step` has been computed\n- manifest updated\n- next action decided\n\n---\n\n## Guardrail 4 — No message-based continuation\nA random incoming user message must never be required to continue an active run.\n\n---\n\n## Guardrail 5 — Retry states must be explicit\nEvery retry must record:\n- retry count\n- retry reason\n- original failure message\n\n---\n\n# 12) Recommended architecture choice\n\n## Best option\nUse a **single durable workflow owner** for the entire run.\n\nThat owner should:\n- own manifest updates\n- own step transitions\n- own process polling\n- own retries\n- only surface meaningful updates to the user\n\n### Why this is best\nBecause your current failure is fundamentally a **workflow ownership problem**.  \nRight now the chat reply boundary is acting like an accidental stop point.\n\nA durable workflow owner removes that weakness.\n\n---\n\n# 13) Minimal acceptable version if architecture stays similar\n\nIf you do not redesign the architecture fully, then at minimum implement these three rules:\n\n### Rule 1\nEvery progress reply must also start the next tool/process in the same turn.\n\n### Rule 2\nEvery long-running exec must be followed by poll/resume handling until terminal result.\n\n### Rule 3\nIf pipeline is active and idle, auto-resume next step from manifest.\n\nThat would probably solve most of the visible stopping behavior.\n\n---\n\n# 14) Validation / test spec\n\nTo confirm the fix works, test these exact scenarios:\n\n## Test A — Happy path\n- start pipeline\n- all steps succeed\n- pipeline reaches Step 5 without any extra user message\n\n### Pass condition\nNo idle pause before WordPress confirmation.\n\n---\n\n## Test B — Approval required during article step\n- article exec requires approval\n\n### Pass condition\n- pipeline pauses only for approval\n- after approval, it resumes automatically from article step\n- does not restart earlier steps\n\n---\n\n## Test C — Research fails once\n- invalid JSON first time\n- valid second time\n\n### Pass condition\n- auto retry happens\n- pipeline continues automatically\n\n---\n\n## Test D — Chart fails twice\n- chart invalid twice\n\n### Pass condition\n- pipeline continues without chart\n- no raw broken placeholder leaks into final export path\n\n---\n\n## Test E — Image generation fails\n### Pass condition\n- pipeline continues to Drive publish\n\n---\n\n## Test F — Publisher fails once then succeeds\n### Pass condition\n- one retry\n- real Drive URL returned\n- reaches Step 5\n\n---\n\n## Test G — Idle recovery\nArtificially force state:\n- `status=active`\n- no running process\n- no input required\n\n### Pass condition\n- workflow auto-detects invalid idle state\n- resumes next step automatically\n\n---\n\n# 15) Definition of done\n\nThis fix is complete only if all of these are true:\n\n- starting the pipeline does not require follow-up “.” messages\n- routine progress messages never leave the run idle\n- run state is explicit and recoverable\n- only approval/blocker/Step 5 can pause execution\n- completion is deterministic\n- resume is state-driven, not message-driven\n\n---\n\n# 16) My final recommendation\n\nIf you want the cleanest implementation:\n\n## Recommended final design\n- explicit manifest-backed state machine\n- single durable workflow owner\n- automatic chaining after every validated step\n- one intentional pause only at WordPress confirmation\n- idle recovery guard as backup\n\nThat is the spec I’d use for a proper “continuous run until end” pipeline.\n\nIf you want, next I can turn this into either:\n\n1. a **developer checklist**\n2. a **step transition table**\n3. a **pseudo-code flow spec**\n\nStill without making any changes."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":3599,"output":4234,"cacheRead":23680,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":31513,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1779097579904,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgoqIWr7p5BzQYAY9KY1fSRRfEBab"}}
